.SH NAME
IC - C compiler for i860\*(tM microprocessor.
.\" End Summary
.SH SYNOPSIS 
.br
\&\fBic\fR\fR\& [\fR\fIfile\fI\fR\&[\fB\&.c\fR\fR\&]] [\fR\fI\
switches\fI\fR\&] 
.SH DESCRIPTION 
.LP
The\fB\& ic\fR\fR\& command invokes the iPSC/860\& C compiler for the Intel i860 microprocessor. The compiler \
is normally accessed using the \fBicc\fR\fR\& driver, which automatically sets many of the compiler's switches. \
Other switches can be passed to \fBic\fR\fR\& using the \fB\&-W0,-\fR\fI\
ds\fI\fR\& of \fBicc\fR\fR\&. For example, the following \fBicc\fR\fR\
\& switch causes \fBic\fR\fR\& to relax data dependency checking and issue a warning for each relaxation:
.PD 0
.RS 0.2i
.nf
.LP
.ta 5.8iR
\&
.br
.br
\fB\&-W0,-depchk,warn\fR
.PD
.RE
.fi
.ta 0.50i +0.5i +0.5i +0.5i +0.5i
.LP
This manual page documents the compiler only; refer to the \fB\
icc\fR\fR\& manual page for information on the driver. 
.LP
The\fB\& ic\fR\fR\& compiler accepts a single C source file as input and normally generates i860 assembly \
language as output. Command line switches let you save the output of the C preprocessor, suppress \
the assembly language output, and create a program listing file.
.LP
If you omit the source file from the command line, the compiler reads its standard input. By default, \
the assembly language appears on the standard output, but a command line switch lets you redirect \
the output to a file.
.LP
If the source file is present, it must end in the suffix ``.c''; however, the ``.c'' suffix can be omitted \
from the filename on the command line. 
.LP
The compiler is targeted to the Intel i860 System Binary Standard (SBS) for the Intel APX system. \
The compiler creates object files and executables that will function for any system conforming to the \
SBS. In addition, the compiler can create object files that will work for any system conforming to \
the System V, Release 4 Application Binary Interface (ABI) for the i860 microprocessor. 
.SH SWITCHES 
.LP
Switches on the command line are processed in the order in which they appear. This means that \
switches that affect other switches must appear before the switches they affect. For example, \fB\
\&-debug\fR\fR\& followed by \fB\&-opt 2\fR\fR\& turns on debug information and sets the optimization level to \fR\fR\
\&2\fR\fR\&, but \fB\&-opt 2\fR\fR\& followed by \fB\&-debug\fR\fR\
\& turns on debug information and sets the optimization level to \fR\fR\
\&0\fR\fR\& (zero). 
.LP
